# T38408 — QinQ - basic functionality — tb470 IE520 stack — 2026-09-30 — PASS

Verdict: PASS. Both steps passed. Step 1: the customer-edge port added the S-tag to every frame
coming in (customer-tagged and untagged, 100/100 each), and removed it from every frame going out
(100/100). Step 2: double-tagged frames arriving on either provider port were switched on the
outer tag. The other provider port and the CE port received them (100/100). They left the other
provider port still double-tagged with the inner tag unchanged (100/100). The inner VID did not
steer forwarding: a decoy VLAN equal to the inner VID got nothing, and a second inner VID (2200)
was switched exactly like 2100. With the feature OFF, the same customer-tagged frames were dropped
(0/100), so the result is due to the QinQ configuration. Run labelled: clean.

The support check at run time passed. The case is port-based QinQ ("configure a port as a customer
edge port / provider port"), and `switchport vlan-stacking customer-edge-port|provider-port` is
offered and accepted on awplus_main-20260923-20. This is not the global VLAN-based `vlan-stacking
vlan … outer-vlan …` that T27887 found absent.

Operator: bench-runner subagent (sentinel = parent session, /test-mode shape A), Terrence away.
Queue: IE520/CAMPAIGN-QUEUE-2026-09-29.md row 7. Case text: ck.db AWPTCM-T38408 (2 steps; objective
"Check QinQ works on the DUT"; precondition "Ixia --CE port DUT Provider port -- Ixia").

## Bench mapping (Ixia -> tb470 NICs through partner switches)
| case role | DUT port | observed / driven by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| CE port (customer side) | port3.0.13 — access vlan 3995 + `switchport vlan-stacking customer-edge-port` | tb470 eth1, direct |
| provider port A | port1.0.13 — trunk, allowed 3995, native 3993, `provider-port` | tb470 eth2 via IE520-sa port1.0.13 (trunk, native 3993, +3995) → SA port1.0.2 (observer trunk, +3995) |
| provider port B | port1.0.2 — trunk, allowed 3995, native 3991, `provider-port` | tb470 eth3 via x230 port1.0.3 (trunk, native 3991, +3995) → x230 port1.0.1 (observer trunk, +3995) |
| decoy (VLAN = the inner VID) | port1.0.9 — access vlan 2100 | tb470 eth3 via x230 port1.0.4 (3992), arriving tagged 3992; plus DUT output counters |
S-VLAN 3995, customer VIDs 2100 and 2200. The partner switches are plain 802.1Q trunks for 3995, so
they pass the outer tag and leave the inner tag alone. They are an observation path, not part of
the device under test.

